claimed: okay-script-page -- the hot-reload half of "a new JSP" (per-request execution + hot-reload, filed in BACKLOG after okay-script-meta). Scope decided narrower than the full backlog entry: a Page(path, classpath) wrapping a render-mode .md file, compiled ONCE and cached by file mtime, re-INVOKED (not re-compiled) on every call -- the actual JSP shape (a page's servlet class compiles once, its _jspService runs once per request). Split ScalaScript.compileAndRun into a compile step (returns an invokable Compiled handle or a Result carrying compile errors) and Compiled.invoke() (the reflection-invoke + stdout-capture step, callable repeatedly without recompiling). request-object injection (JSP's implicit request/response) is NOT in scope this pass -- filed as a separate follow-on, since it would add a real okay-http dependency to okay-script's own main code, which the backlog entry's own phrasing ("closer to an okay-jetty route than to okay-script itself") argues against doing lightly. Page itself needs no new dependency -- it only wraps ScalaScript.render + file mtime.
